.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt ASN1_TYPE_GET 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ASN1_TYPE_get ,
.Nm ASN1_TYPE_set ,
.Nm ASN1_TYPE_set1 ,
.Nm ASN1_TYPE_cmp ,
.Nm ASN1_TYPE_unpack_sequence ,
.Nm ASN1_TYPE_pack_sequence
.Nd ASN1_TYPE utility functions
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/asn1.h
.Ft int
.Fo ASN1_TYPE_get
.Fa "const ASN1_TYPE *a"
.Fc
.Ft void
.Fo ASN1_TYPE_set
.Fa "ASN1_TYPE *a"
.Fa "int type"
.Fa "void *value"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo ASN1_TYPE_set1
.Fa "ASN1_TYPE *a"
.Fa "int type"
.Fa "const void *value"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo ASN1_TYPE_cmp
.Fa "const ASN1_TYPE *a"
.Fa "const ASN1_TYPE *b"
.Fc
.Ft void *
.Fo ASN1_TYPE_unpack_sequence
.Fa "const ASN1_ITEM *it"
.Fa "const ASN1_TYPE *t"
.Fc
.Ft ASN1_TYPE *
.Fo ASN1_TYPE_pack_sequence
.Fa "const ASN1_ITEM *it"
.Fa "void *s"
.Fa "ASN1_TYPE **t"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These functions allow an
.Vt ASN1_TYPE
structure to be manipulated.
The
.Vt ASN1_TYPE
structure can contain any ASN.1 type or constructed type such as a
SEQUENCE: it is effectively equivalent to the ASN.1 ANY type.
.Pp
.Fn ASN1_TYPE_get
returns the type of
.Fa a
or 0 if it fails.
.Pp
.Fn ASN1_TYPE_set
sets the value of
.Fa a
to
.Fa type
and
.Fa value .
This function uses the pointer
.Fa value
internally so it must
.Sy not
be freed up after the call.
.Pp
.Fn ASN1_TYPE_set1
sets the value of
.Fa a
to
.Fa type
a copy of
.Fa value .
.Pp
.Fn ASN1_TYPE_cmp
compares ASN.1 types
.Fa a
and
.Fa b
and returns 0 if they are identical and nonzero otherwise.
.Pp
.Fn ASN1_TYPE_unpack_sequence
attempts to parse the SEQUENCE present in
.Fa t
using the ASN.1 structure
.Fa it .
If successful it returns a pointer to the ASN.1 structure corresponding
to
.Fa it
which must be freed by the caller.
If it fails it return NULL.
.Pp
.Fn ASN1_TYPE_pack_sequence
attempts to encode the ASN.1 structure
.Fa s
corresponding to
.Fa it
into an
.Vt ASN1_TYPE .
If successful the encoded
.Vt ASN1_TYPE
is returned.
If
.Fa t
and
.Em *t
are not NULL the encoded type is written to
.Fa t
overwriting any existing data.
If
.Fa t
is not NULL but
.Em *t
is NULL the returned
.Vt ASN1_TYPE
is written to
.Em *t .
.Sh NOTES
The type and meaning of the
.Fa value
parameter for
.Fn ASN1_TYPE_set
and
.Fn ASN1_TYPE_set1
is determined by the
.Fa type
parameter.
If
.Fa type
is
.Sy V_ASN1_NULL
.Fa value
is ignored.
If
.Fa type
is
.Sy V_ASN1_BOOLEAN
then the boolean is set to TRUE if
.Fa value
is not NULL. If
.Fa type
is
.Sy V_ASN1_OBJECT
then value is an
.Sy ASN1_OBJECT
structure.
Otherwise
.Fa type
is and
.Sy ASN1_STRING
structure.
If
.Fa type
corresponds to a primitive type (or a string type) then the contents of
the
.Sy ASN1_STRING
contain the content octets of the type.
If
.Fa type
corresponds to a constructed type or a tagged type
.Pf ( Sy V_ASN1_SEQUENCE ,
.Sy V_ASN1_SET
or
.Sy V_ASN1_OTHER )
then the
.Sy ASN1_STRING
contains the entire ASN.1 encoding verbatim (including tag and length
octets).
.Pp
.Fn ASN1_TYPE_cmp
may not return zero if two types are equivalent but have different
encodings.
For example the single content octet of the boolean TRUE value under BER
can have any nonzero encoding but
.Fn ASN1_TYPE_cmp
will only return zero if the values are the same.
.Pp
If either or both of the parameters passed to
.Fn ASN1_TYPE_cmp
is NULL the return value is nonzero.
Technically if both parameters are NULL the two types could be absent
OPTIONAL fields and so should match, however, passing NULL values could
also indicate a programming error (for example an unparsable type which
returns NULL) for types which do
.Sy not
match.
So applications should handle the case of two absent values separately.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn ASN1_TYPE_get
returns the type of the
.Vt ASN1_TYPE
argument.
.Pp
.Fn ASN1_TYPE_set
does not return a value.
.Pp
.Fn ASN1_TYPE_set1
returns 1 for success and 0 for failure.
.Pp
.Fn ASN1_TYPE_cmp
returns 0 if the types are identical and nonzero otherwise.
.Pp
.Fn ASN1_TYPE_unpack_sequence
returns a pointer to an ASN.1 structure or NULL on failure.
.Pp
.Fn ASN1_TYPE_pack_sequence
return an
.Vt ASN1_TYPE
structure if it succeeds or NULL on failure.
